{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>At about midnight I go out in the midst of Egypt.</b> Rashi explained in parashat Shemot (Exodus 2:11) on the verse ״וַיַּרְא אִישׁ מִצְרִי מַכֶּה״ etc., that the taskmasters roused Israel to their labor at crack of dawn. And from where did Rashi learn to say that they roused them specifically at crack of dawn? And perhaps Rashi learned this from what is said, ״כַּחֲצוֹת הַלַּיְלָה״ etc., for we find punishments for Egypt measure for measure in all ten plagues. But we have not yet found a punishment from the aspect of time; therefore it says ״כַּחֲצוֹת הַלַּיְלָה״ with a כַּ״ף — like that very midnight when they did them wrong and violence to rouse them to their labor at crack of dawn, which is the first watch, which is at midnight, and the taskmasters were great officers; so the firstborn of Egypt were smitten as well at midnight. Or it refers to that very midnight of Abraham, just as Abraham smote the kings at midnight, so the firstborn of Egypt were smitten at midnight." "And what He wrote, ״אֲנִי יוֹצֵא״ — this is to tell you that at first the Holy One, blessed be He, went out Himself in order to cast down the prince of Egypt, for this matter has no power in the hand of any emissary or angel to do, and after this HaShem sent destroyers against the firstborn of Egypt. Therefore it says ״אֲנִי יוֹצֵא בְּתוֹךְ מִצְרָיִם״ and He did not say ״בְּתוֹךְ אֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם״, and afterward He said ״וּמֵת כָּל בְּכוֹר בְּאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם״. Rather, surely the first going-out was not in the land but in heaven, and with this the entire difficulty of the commentators is resolved — for how did they say regarding the plague of the firstborn ״אֲנִי וְלֹא מַלְאָךְ״ etc., and yet Scripture says ״וְלֹא יִתֵּן הַמַּשְׁחִית לָבֹא אֶל בָּתֵּיכֶם״ (Exodus 12:23)? Rather, it is clear as we have stated. And He used the expression ״יוֹצֵא״ in the manner of what is said (Isaiah 26:21) ״כִּי הִנֵּה ה׳ יֹצֵא מִמְּקוֹמוֹ״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted (Jerusalem Talmud, Ta'anit 2:1): He goes out from the throne of mercy and sits upon the throne of judgment. Thus the going-out here is to execute judgment upon their gods first, as He wrote (Isaiah 34:5) ״כִּי רִוְּתָה בַשָּׁמַיִם חַרְבִּי״, and afterward ״עַל אֱדוֹם תֵּרֵד״. So too is the matter here, and this is a precious resolution."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "The word קְרוֹת הַגֶּבֶר (lit. 'calls of the dawn') refers to the dawn watch when taskmasters roused the slaves; rendered as 'crack of dawn' throughout."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:19:30.315524235Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}